@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Introduction to DevOps";
    ViewData["ActiveMenu"] = "AzureDevOps";
}

<div class="container py-4">
    <h1 class="display-4 text-center">Getting Started with DevOps</h1>
    <hr />

    <!-- What is DevOps? -->
    <div class="row align-items-center mt-4">
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h2>What is DevOps?</h2>
            <p>
                DevOps is a <strong>culture</strong>, <strong>set of practices</strong>, and <strong>tools</strong> that improve collaboration between software development (Dev) and IT operations (Ops) teams. 
                The goal is to shorten the development lifecycle, deliver high-quality software faster, and ensure reliable deployments.
            </p>
            <p>Think of DevOps as a bridge between traditionally separate teams:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Development Team:</strong> Writes code and builds new features.</li>
                <li><strong>Operations Team:</strong> Manages servers, deploys software, and ensures systems run smoothly.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>DevOps enables these teams to work as a single unit focused on delivering value to the end user.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="~/css/Images/AzureDevOps/DevOps fosters collaboration between Dev and Ops teams.webp"
                alt="DevOps fosters collaboration between Dev and Ops teams"
                class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>DevOps fosters collaboration between Dev and Ops teams.</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- DevOps vs Traditional IT -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>DevOps vs Traditional IT</h2>
        <p>In traditional IT, development and operations teams work in silos. Here’s how DevOps differs:</p>
        <table class="table table-bordered text-center">
            <thead class="table-dark">
                <tr>
                    <th>Aspect</th>
                    <th>Traditional IT</th>
                    <th>DevOps</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Team Structure</strong></td>
                    <td>Separate teams for Dev and Ops</td>
                    <td>Collaborative, cross-functional teams</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Communication</strong></td>
                    <td>Limited communication</td>
                    <td>Continuous collaboration and feedback</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Deployment</strong></td>
                    <td>Manual, infrequent deployments</td>
                    <td>Automated, frequent deployments</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Focus</strong></td>
                    <td>Stability and control</td>
                    <td>Speed, agility, and innovation</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <!-- Benefits of DevOps -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Benefits of DevOps</h2>
        <p>Adopting DevOps brings numerous benefits:</p>
        <ul class="list-group list-group-flush">
            <li class="list-group-item"><strong>🚀 Faster Delivery:</strong> Automated processes speed up software releases (e.g., Netflix deploys thousands of times per day).</li>
            <li class="list-group-item"><strong>🤝 Improved Collaboration:</strong> DevOps fosters a culture of shared responsibility using tools like Slack & Microsoft Teams.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item"><strong>✅ Higher Quality:</strong> Continuous testing ensures fewer bugs (e.g., Selenium, JUnit).</li>
            <li class="list-group-item"><strong>📈 Scalability:</strong> Cloud platforms like AWS & Azure enable scaling infrastructure efficiently.</li>
            <li class="list-group-item"><strong>🔄 Reliability:</strong> Automated deployments reduce errors, ensuring system stability (e.g., Jenkins, GitLab CI/CD).</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- DevOps Lifecycle -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>DevOps Lifecycle</h2>
        <p>The DevOps lifecycle consists of these continuous stages:</p>
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col-md-6">
                <ol>
                    <li><strong>Plan:</strong> Define goals & roadmap (Jira, Trello).</li>
                    <li><strong>Code:</strong> Developers write code (Git, GitHub, GitLab).</li>
                    <li><strong>Build:</strong> Compile & package code (Maven, Gradle).</li>
                    <li><strong>Test:</strong> Automated testing ensures quality (Selenium, JUnit).</li>
                </ol>
            </div>
            <div class="col-md-6">
                <ol start="5">
                    <li><strong>Release:</strong> Deploy to staging for testing (Jenkins, CircleCI).</li>
                    <li><strong>Deploy:</strong> Release to production (Kubernetes, Docker).</li>
                    <li><strong>Operate:</strong> Monitor system performance (Nagios, Prometheus).</li>
                    <li><strong>Monitor:</strong> Log & analyze metrics (Splunk, ELK Stack).</li>
                </ol>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Learning Outcomes -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Learning Outcomes</h2>
        <p>By the end of this module, you will:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Understand the fundamentals of DevOps and its significance.</li>
            <li>Learn the key differences between DevOps & Traditional IT.</li>
            <li>Explore the benefits of DevOps adoption.</li>
            <li>Gain insights into the DevOps lifecycle stages.</li>
            <li>Get familiar with popular DevOps tools & best practices.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>
